  "agent": {
    "note": "**M2.3 — character-level parse model (back to the drawing board).** You were right: m0 parses one CHARACTER at a time, and every char is a real step. `2(3[1,1,1],2[1,1])` is now **18 steps** — one per character — and the X/Y/W/H mutation happens *on the exact char that causes it*:\n\n| step | char | op | effect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `2` | count | read divisor — no geometry change |\n| 2 | `(` | **split** | **W → 960** (halves the canvas) |\n| 3 | `3` | count | read divisor |\n| 4 | `[` | **split** | **H → 360** (divides by 3) |\n| 5 | `1` | emit | paint tile |\n| 6 | `,` | sibling | **Y → 360** (next row) |\n| … | | | |\n| 10 | `]` | close | **restore** to the column `960×1080` |\n| 11 | `,` | sibling | **X → 960** (second column) |\n| 18 | `)` | close | restore to full canvas |\n\n**Opens** split the canvas, **commas** move to the next sibling, **closes** restore to the parent — exactly as you described. Rects come from the real parse tree, so they're exact.\n\n**Z = overlay depth** now (nesting inside `{}`), not logical index — so it's **0** for every tile here, as it should be. The painted frame number already shows the index; Z is reserved for overlay layering.\n\n_(Process: I also caught up the `.mosaic` provenance freeze I'd regressed on — this candidate has its `.mosaic` twin frozen beside it.)_",
    "question": "Does the char-level model read right now? Checks: (a) 18 steps, one per char, opening on Step 0 (empty frame), (b) W halves on the `(` and H divides on the `[` — not a step early/late, (c) commas advance the cursor (Y then X), closes restore to the parent, (d) Z stays 0. If the parse model is faithful, I'll move to **M3 — the DSL-string panel** (syntax highlight + a caret that sits on the current char + narration), which makes this char-by-char story explicit on screen.",
